sonline.com/blogs/best-uses-random-wheel-spinner#breadcrumbs\"\n      },\n      \"speakable\": {\n        \"@type\": \"SpeakableSpecification\",\n        \"cssSelector\": [\n          \"h1\",\n          \".quick-answer\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"BreadcrumbList\",\n      \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/best-uses-random-wheel-spinner#breadcrumbs\",\n      \"itemListElement\": [\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"ListItem\",\n          \"position\": 1,\n          \"name\": \"Home\",\n          \"item\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"ListItem\",\n          \"position\": 2,\n          \"name\": \"Blog\",\n          \"item\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"ListItem\",\n          \"position\": 3,\n          \"name\": \"10 Creative Ways to Use a Spin Wheel\",\n          \"item\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/best-uses-random-wheel-spinner\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"BlogPosting\",\n      \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/best-uses-random-wheel-spinner#article\",\n      \"headline\": \"10 Creative Ways to Use a Spin Wheel (Not Just Giveaways)\",\n      \"description\": \"Ten practical spin wheel setups for writing, workouts, budgeting, meals, studying, chores and daily tasks, with the exact entries to load.\",\n      \"image\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/eng-spin-the-wheel-picker.webp\",\n      \"datePublished\": \"2026-08-16\",\n      \"dateModified\": \"2026-11-24\",\n      \"inLanguage\": \"en\",\n      \"author\": {\n        \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/#harman\"\n      },\n      \"publisher\": {\n        \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/#organization\"\n      },\n      \"mainEntityOfPage\": {\n        \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/best-uses-random-wheel-spinner#webpage\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"FAQPage\",\n      \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/best-uses-random-wheel-spinner#faq\",\n      \"mainEntity\": [\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"What is a spin wheel actually good for besides giveaways?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"A spin wheel is good for any decision where you already have a shortlist of acceptable options and just need to commit. Common examples are choosing a workout, a dinner, a writing prompt, a chore assignment, the next task to work on, or a study item to review.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"How many entries should I put on a wheel?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"For everyday decisions, 4 to 12 entries works well: enough variety to feel worth spinning, few enough that you would accept any result. Study drills can use 15 to 30 entries, and Spin The Wheels Online supports up to 500 names or choices per wheel.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"Can I keep separate wheels for different uses?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"Yes. Every wheel you build gets its own shareable link, and Save Custom Wheel lets you store named wheels locally in your browser's LocalStorage—so you can switch between workouts, date nights, and study prompts without rebuilding anything.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"How is a spin wheel different from a Yes or No wheel?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"A standard wheel accepts any number of custom entries up to 500. A Yes or No wheel is designed around binary go or no-go calls (or Yes / No / Maybe presets) rather than choosing among many distinct options.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"Should I follow the result if I don't like it?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"If you only put in options you would accept, following the result should be easy. A strong reaction against the result is useful information: it usually means one of the entries was not really acceptable, so remove it and spin again.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"Do I need an account to use these setups?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"No accounts exist or are required. You can build a wheel, save multiple named wheels locally in your browser's LocalStorage, export results, and share ?w= links completely free.\"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}"} />

