          <h2 id="intro">What a Wheel Actually Does For You</h2>
          <p>
            A spin wheel does not come up with your options. That part is still
            yours. What it removes is the last and most draining step of a
            decision: choosing one item from a list of things you would already
            be fine with. That final step is where people tend to stall,
            re-compare and second-guess, even though nothing much is at stake.
          </p>
          <p>
            Seen that way, the use cases go far beyond giveaways and picking
            names in class. Anywhere you have a shortlist and a reluctance to
            commit, a wheel ends the stalling. If you are new to the tool
            itself, start with our explainer on
            <a href="/blogs/spin-the-wheel-tool">what a spin the wheel tool is and how it works</a>,
            then come back here for the practical setups. If you want to know
            why handing small choices to chance feels so relieving, we cover
            that in
            <a href="/blogs/psychology-random-decision-making">the psychology behind random decision making</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="build">How to Build a Wheel Worth Spinning</h2>
          <p>
            Every setup below follows the same five rules. They are the
            difference between a wheel you use for months and one you abandon
            after a single spin.
          </p>
          <ol class="plain">
            <li>
              <strong>Filter first, spin second.</strong> Only add options you
              would genuinely accept. A wheel full of things you do not want
              simply stacks a coin flip on top of an unmade decision.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Keep it short.</strong> Four to twelve entries is the
              practical range for everyday choices. Longer lists make each
              entry feel arbitrary and invite you to skip the result.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Decide the rule before the spin.</strong> Agree that the
              result is binding, or agree in advance that you get one re-spin.
              Negotiating afterwards defeats the point.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Use several small wheels, not one big one.</strong> A
              "type" wheel plus a "duration" wheel gives more variety, and more
              honest results, than one tangled list.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Save it as a link.</strong> Each wheel you build has its
              own shareable link, so a wheel you use weekly is one bookmark
              away instead of being rebuilt every time.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <div class="box box-info">
            <div class="box-label">A useful test</div>
            <p>
              Read your entries aloud and imagine each one coming up. If any
              result would make you say "not that one," delete it now. That
              reaction is your real preference showing, and the wheel cannot
              fix it for you.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- USE 1 -->
          <section class="use-card uc-1" id="use-1">
            <div class="uc-header">
              <div class="uc-badge" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
              <div>
                <h3>✍️ Breaking a Writing Block</h3>
                <p class="uc-tagline">
                  A constraint moves you forward faster than a blank page
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              Writer's block is rarely a shortage of ideas. It is usually too
              many half-formed ideas and no obvious way to rank them. A wheel
              skips the ranking. You spin, you get a starting point, and the
              choosing is over before it can slow you down.
            </p>
            <p>
              Instead of one long list, build three small wheels: a setting
              wheel, a character-flaw wheel and a conflict wheel. Spin all
              three and combine what lands. The pairings you would never have
              chosen on purpose tend to be the most interesting, because you
              cannot fall back on your usual instincts. For character names,
              pair the exercise with our
              <a href="/random-name-generator">random name generator</a>.
            </p>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Entries to try</strong>
              Setting: an abandoned lighthouse, an overnight train, a family
              restaurant in its final week. Flaw: can't admit mistakes, always
              late, hoards secrets. Conflict: a promise that can't be kept, a
              stranger who knows too much, a deadline that moved without
              warning.
            </div>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Common mistake</strong>
              Re-spinning until you get a combination you like. Give yourself
              one re-spin at most; the discomfort of a strange pairing is the
              whole benefit.
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- USE 2 -->
          <section class="use-card uc-2" id="use-2">
            <div class="uc-header">
              <div class="uc-badge" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
              <div>
                <h3>💪 Keeping a Workout Routine From Going Stale</h3>
                <p class="uc-tagline">
                  Take the "what am I even doing today" decision off the table
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              A fixed weekly split is easy to follow for a month and easy to
              quietly drop after that, usually once you can predict
              Wednesday's session before it happens. Predictability is
              efficient, but it is also where motivation leaks out.
            </p>
            <p>
              Set up two wheels, one for workout type and one for duration, and
              spin both <em>before</em> you change clothes. Deciding in advance
              removes the chance to talk yourself out of it after seeing the
              result. Treat "rest" as a normal entry: if the wheel picked it,
              it counts. You can also let the
              <a href="/sport-picker-wheel">sport picker wheel</a> choose a
              sport for the weekend, or use the
              <a href="/day-picker-wheel">day picker wheel</a> to decide
              which day gets your hardest session. For a wider weekly system,
              see
              <a href="/blogs/how-to-use-spin-wheel-plan-week-meals-workouts-tasks">how to plan a week of meals, workouts and tasks with a wheel</a>.
            </p>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Entries to try</strong>
              Type: intervals, strength, mobility, long walk, swim, rest.
              Duration: 20 min, 35 min, 50 min. Six types times three
              durations gives 18 combinations before any repeat.
            </div>
          </section>

