          <!-- USE 3 -->
          <section class="use-card uc-3" id="use-3">
            <div class="uc-header">
              <div class="uc-badge" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
              <div>
                <h3>💰 Closing Out Discretionary Spending</h3>
                <p class="uc-tagline">
                  The budget is done; the wheel just closes the loop
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              This one is for a narrow situation: you have already set a
              spending cap and shortlisted several things that all fit inside
              it, yet you keep circling back to compare them instead of buying
              anything. The hesitation is no longer about money. It is about
              not wanting to feel you picked the "wrong" option.
            </p>
            <p>
              Because every entry already fits your budget, there is no wrong
              outcome. Load the options, fix the cap once, spin, and stop
              revisiting the decision. This only works if the shortlist is
              honest. Never add something you cannot comfortably afford just to
              see what happens. When you are down to exactly two equal
              options, the
              <a href="/coin-flip-wheel">coin flip wheel</a> does the same
              job faster.
            </p>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Entries to try</strong>
              This month's "extra" fund: a day trip, new running shoes, one
              nice dinner out, a book haul. Set the cap, spin, spend, and do
              not reopen the question afterward.
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- USE 4 -->
          <section class="use-card uc-4" id="use-4">
            <div class="uc-header">
              <div class="uc-badge" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
              <div>
                <h3>🍽️ Ending the "Where Should We Eat" Loop</h3>
                <p class="uc-tagline">
                  Cut the exchange off before it starts
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              You know the exchange: "Where do you want to eat?" "I don't mind,
              you pick." Ten minutes pass and nothing is decided. A shared
              wheel, saved as a link both people can open, cuts it short
              because the result is agreed to be binding before anyone spins.
              The <a href="/food-picker-wheel">food picker wheel</a> gives
              you a ready-made set of cuisines, and our guide on
              <a href="/blogs/what-to-eat-tonight-spin-wheel-dinner-decision">deciding what to eat tonight with a wheel</a>
              shows how to tailor it to your usual haunts.
            </p>
            <p>
              The same method scales to bigger choices. Shortlist destinations
              that are genuinely within budget and time, then let the
              <a href="/country-picker-wheel">country picker wheel</a> or
              your own list decide. For an evening out, couples can combine a
              dinner wheel with a film wheel; see our
              <a href="/blogs/date-night-roulette-spin-wheel-ideas-couples">date night roulette ideas</a>
              and the <a href="/movie-genre-wheel">movie genre wheel</a>.
            </p>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Entries to try</strong>
              Dinner: your usual six go-tos, filtered to what you would both
              enjoy tonight. Travel: five to eight destinations within your
              real budget and available dates, nothing aspirational you would
              talk yourself out of anyway.
            </div>
          </section>

