          <!-- USE 5 -->
          <section class="use-card uc-5" id="use-5">
            <div class="uc-header">
              <div class="uc-badge" aria-hidden="true">05</div>
              <div>
                <h3>🧘 Getting Past the Blank Page in Journaling</h3>
                <p class="uc-tagline">A specific prompt beats an open one</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              "Write about your day" is hard to start from because it does not
              point anywhere. A specific, slightly unexpected prompt does. Part
              of the reason is that it is specific, and part is that you did
              not choose it, so there is nothing to perform for.
            </p>
            <p>
              Load a wheel with reflective prompts and spin <em>before</em> you
              start writing, not after ten minutes of stalling. Set a timer for
              five to ten minutes and write only about what landed. Refresh
              the list every month so the prompts do not become predictable.
            </p>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Entries to try</strong>
              "A moment this week you felt proud of, however small." "Something
              you are avoiding, and why." "What rest actually looks like for
              you right now." "A decision you are glad you made."
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- USE 6 -->
          <section class="use-card uc-6" id="use-6">
            <div class="uc-header">
              <div class="uc-badge" aria-hidden="true">06</div>
              <div>
                <h3>📚 Turning Flashcard Review Into a Real Drill</h3>
                <p class="uc-tagline">
                  Unpredictable order beats a deck whose sequence you have
                  memorized
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              Flashcard apps work well until you start recognizing the order of
              the deck instead of genuinely recalling each answer. A wheel has
              no order to memorize, which keeps recall honest. This builds on
              what learning researchers call the testing effect: actively
              retrieving an answer strengthens memory more than rereading it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Load vocabulary, verb forms or formulas, set a hard limit of
              about five seconds per answer, and only remove an entry when you
              got it right without hesitating. Anything left after a few passes
              is exactly what needs more study. Language learners can pair
              this with the
              <a href="/language-picker-wheel">language picker wheel</a> to
              choose what to practice, and our guides on
              <a href="/blogs/how-students-use-spin-wheels-study-revision-focus">student study and revision setups</a>
              and
              <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-language-learning-vocabulary-verbs-conversation">language learning with a wheel</a>
              go deeper.
            </p>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Entries to try</strong>
              Fifteen to twenty current vocabulary words. Spin, answer within
              five seconds, remove it only if you were right and fast.
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- USE 7 -->
          <section class="use-card uc-7" id="use-7">
            <div class="uc-header">
              <div class="uc-badge" aria-hidden="true">07</div>
              <div>
                <h3>🏠 Splitting Chores Without Arguing</h3>
                <p class="uc-tagline">
                  One spin beats the same negotiation every Sunday
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              Most chore disagreements are not really about the chore. They are
              about who decided the assignment and whether it felt fair. A
              shared wheel removes the "who decided" question because nobody
              did; it landed where it landed.
            </p>
            <p>
              Put each of the week's tasks on the wheel, spin once per person,
              and treat the result as final. Over many weeks chance tends to
              even things out, though a single week can feel lopsided. If one
              person keeps drawing the task everyone dislikes, add a simple
              rule such as "nobody gets the same chore twice in a row." For
              splitting a group into fair sides, the
              <a href="/team-picker-wheel">team picker wheel</a> handles it
              in one spin.
            </p>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Entries to try</strong>
              This week's real chores: dishes, bins, laundry, grocery run,
              bathroom clean. Rebuild the wheel each week instead of reusing
              last week's list.
            </div>
          </section>

          <figure class="inline-image">
            <img
              src="/wheel-settings.webp"
