          <!-- USE 8 -->
          <section class="use-card uc-8" id="use-8">
            <div class="uc-header">
              <div class="uc-badge" aria-hidden="true">08</div>
              <div>
                <h3>🔬 Unsticking a Brainstorm With a Forced Constraint</h3>
                <p class="uc-tagline">
                  An unrelated input beats a comfortable one
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              Random stimulation is an established creativity technique, often
              associated with Edward de Bono's work on lateral thinking: bring
              in a concept unrelated to the problem, then force a connection.
              It works because stuck brainstorms tend to circle the same few
              familiar directions, and an unrelated word breaks that pattern in
              a way "just think differently" never does.
            </p>
            <p>
              A wheel is the simplest way to pick that word on demand. Load it
              with plain nouns, spin whenever the room goes quiet, and give the
              group a fixed five minutes to link the word to the problem. For
              visual projects, the
              <a href="/color-picker-wheel">color picker wheel</a> can
              supply a palette constraint. Teams can find more formats in our
              guides to
              <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-team-building">team building with a wheel</a>
              and
              <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-icebreakers-onboarding">icebreakers and onboarding</a>.
            </p>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Entries to try</strong>
              Umbrella, greenhouse, subway map, library card, campfire.
              Question for five minutes: what does this word suggest for a
              feature, a name, or how we would explain this to a customer?
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- USE 9 -->
          <section class="use-card uc-9" id="use-9">
            <div class="uc-header">
              <div class="uc-badge" aria-hidden="true">09</div>
              <div>
                <h3>📱 Filling a Content Calendar in One Sitting</h3>
                <p class="uc-tagline">
                  Two short spins beat an hour of staring at an empty grid
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              If you post regularly, the hard part is rarely having topics. It
              is deciding, fresh every day, which topic and which format to
              use. That daily re-deciding is what drains people.
            </p>
            <p>
              Do the deciding once a week. Build a format wheel and a topic
              wheel for your niche, spin both several times in one sitting, and
              fill the week's calendar with the pairings. You are still
              choosing what to post, just once instead of daily. Creators who
              go live can also give viewers a wheel to spin on stream; see how
              <a href="/blogs/streamers-use-spin-wheel">streamers use spin wheels</a>.
            </p>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Entries to try</strong>
              Format: tutorial, hot take, behind the scenes, Q&amp;A,
              before/after. Topic: your six to eight recurring subject areas.
              Spin both seven times on Sunday for the whole week.
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- USE 10 -->
          <section class="use-card uc-10" id="use-10">
            <div class="uc-header">
              <div class="uc-badge" aria-hidden="true">10</div>
              <div>
                <h3>⚡ Working a Task List Without Re-Ranking It</h3>
                <p class="uc-tagline">
                  Stop reorganizing the list and start working it
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              A lot of procrastination is not avoiding work. It is avoiding the
              decision about which piece of work to start with, especially when
              everything feels equally urgent. Reordering a to-do list can feel
              productive while achieving nothing.
            </p>
            <p>
              Add the day's tasks to a wheel and spin at the start of each
              focus block. Work on whatever lands for a set stretch, with 25
              minutes being the common Pomodoro length, then spin again. It
              will not always give the "optimal" order, but it reliably beats
              the time lost deciding what optimal means. Genuinely urgent items
              (a deadline today) should be done first and kept off the wheel.
              For smaller "now or later" calls during the day, use the
              <a href="/yes-no-wheel">Yes or No wheel</a>, and read
              <a href="/blogs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use">when a Yes or No wheel is the right tool</a>.
            </p>
            <div class="uc-example">
              <strong>Entries to try</strong>
              Six to ten real tasks for today. Spin at the start of each
              25-minute block. Whatever lands is the block; no renegotiating
              once it is spun.
            </div>
          </section>

          <div class="box box-purple">
            <div class="box-label">What all ten have in common</div>
            <p>
              In every setup, the thinking happened before the spin: the
              shortlist, the budget, the acceptable options. The wheel does
              not decide for you. It ends the loop of re-deciding something
              you had already settled. That is the real value of a random
              wheel: not luck, but a clean way to commit.
            </p>
          </div>

