        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What is a spin wheel actually good for besides giveaways?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              A spin wheel is good for any decision where you already have a
              shortlist of acceptable options and just need to commit. Common
              examples are choosing a workout, a dinner, a writing prompt, a
              chore assignment, the next task to work on, or a study item to
              review.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>How many entries should I put on a wheel?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              For everyday decisions, 4 to 12 entries works well: enough variety to feel worth spinning, few enough that you would accept any result. Study drills often use 15 to 30 entries, and when picking from large groups our wheel supports up to 500 entries cleanly.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Can I keep separate wheels for different uses?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              Yes. Every wheel you build gets its own shareable link, so you
              can keep one for workouts, one for date nights and one for
              content ideas, and switch between them without rebuilding
              anything.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>How is a spin wheel different from a Yes or No wheel?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              A standard wheel accepts any number of custom entries. A Yes or
              No wheel is limited to two outcomes and is meant for quick go or
              no-go calls rather than choosing among several options.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Should I follow the result if I don't like it?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              If you only put in options you would accept, following the result
              should be easy. A strong reaction against the result is useful
              information: it usually means one of the entries was not really
              acceptable, so remove it and spin again.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Do I need an account to use these setups?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              No. No account exists or is required—you can build a wheel, spin it, and share via <code>?w=</code> link without signing up. Custom wheels save locally inside your browser's <code>localStorage</code> so frequent lists stay ready on reload.
            </div>
          </details>

          <div class="box box-success">
            <div class="box-label">Quick recap</div>
            <ul>
              <li>
                A wheel is useful anywhere you have a pre-approved shortlist
                and need to stop stalling.
              </li>
              <li>
                Filter your options honestly first. The wheel removes the last
                step, not the thinking.
              </li>
              <li>
                Use several small wheels with 4 to 12 entries instead of one
                big list.
              </li>
              <li>
                Save each setup as a link so it is ready in seconds, and skip
                the wheel for high-stakes choices.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>


